# base10-numeral.iregexp
#
# VERBATIM -- the single regular expression RFC 9741 prints. It appears
# inline in §2.2 prose at source line 252, not as a display block, so
# there is no margin to preserve; the one line below is the regexp as
# printed and nothing else. The sentence that carries it is
# rfc-9741#R-0024 in ../requirements.yaml.
#
# DIALECT: I-Regexp (RFC 9485), per §1.1 - "Regular expressions
# mentioned in the text are as defined in [RFC9485]". NOT the XSD
# dialect that RFC 8610 §3.8.3 gives .regexp. OURS, and UNVERIFIED:
# the two dialects differ, and this expression is valid in both. This
# extraction did not read RFC 9485 or the XSD specification.
#
# It is the canonical decimal form .base10 admits: no leading zeros, an
# optional minus for a non-zero value, and a bare 0 for zero. It is
# a whole-string match - OURS and UNVERIFIED, from I-Regexp being
# anchored; RFC 9485 was not read here - and it is
# further narrowed by the controller type.
#
# Source: RFC 9741 §2.2, cached source sha256
#   3234aeea39d01b7c1c8e9c70d4d7dc8745852e0b6edbea167a3e55b91e7a9ee3
0|-?[1-9][0-9]*
